{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001280","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to pulmonary embolism, but they split into (a) a coherent UKB snpnet pan-trait framework cluster (PGS001277–PGS001280) with explicit PRS-only metrics and basic covariates, (b) an EAS-only disease-focused PE study (PGS003861) reporting only a full-model AUROC with unknown covariates, and (c) two 2024 UKB genome-wide scores (PGS004460/PGS004530) with strong variant count/method signals but essentially no comparable performance metrics beyond a single OR.

PGS001280 is preferred within the best-supported evidence cluster because it has exact PE EFO/endpoint (trait_efo and phenotyping_reported: pulmonary embolism / TTE PE) and the strongest full-model AUROC among the snpnet siblings (0.67617, European validation) while still providing PRS-only metrics (PGS AUROC 0.60765; PGS R2 0.01403) under clean, comparable covariates (age, sex, array, PCs). The PRS-only R2 is also plausibly below the provided PE heritability ceiling signals, supporting internal consistency.

Main limitation: the snpnet models come from a pan-trait UKB framework paper (domain knowledge flags framework+sparse methods as generally weaker for external generalization), while the methodologically stronger 2024 genome-wide candidates lack PRS-comparable performance reporting here, preventing a high-confidence selection. Therefore the direct match is present but the best choice is not unequivocally supported from the visible metadata."}
